WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:00.365
Good morning John,

00:00:00.465 --> 00:00:01.289
I'm about to sing you a song,

00:00:01.389 --> 00:00:03.140
but first I have 
a couple of things to tell you.

00:00:03.240 --> 00:00:05.919
First: my hands are kinda permanently red.
And you'll find out why later.

00:00:06.019 --> 00:00:08.390
Second: mostly, I want to say thank you
to everyone who helped me out with this.

00:00:08.490 --> 00:00:10.560
There are a lot of different parts in this
song,

00:00:10.660 --> 00:00:13.770
but I can't play any of them,
so I had Nerdfighters do it for me.

00:00:13.870 --> 00:00:18.029
So, thank you to Paige, Derling,
Danielle, Elisiah, Morgan and Kevin

00:00:18.129 --> 00:00:21.099
for all of your amazing Irish fiddling
and penny whistle playing.

00:00:21.199 --> 00:00:23.269
I'm sorry I couldn't use everybody's;
there were some problems with sound

00:00:23.369 --> 00:00:26.579
and with actually getting the video
 to work in my computer,

00:00:26.679 --> 00:00:29.100
so I just used what I could use.
Thank you to all of you;

00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:30.140
you did an amazing job.

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:31.279
Lastly, before I share this song,

00:00:31.379 --> 00:00:33.610
I want to talk a little bit about squirrel
linguistics.

00:00:33.710 --> 00:00:35.370
You may or may not know this, 
but squirrels -

00:00:35.470 --> 00:00:36.135
they speak.

00:00:36.235 --> 00:00:38.299
[Imitating squirrel chattering]
Cruchla crcruchler chrr crurlarr -

00:00:38.399 --> 00:00:39.240
little squirrel noises.

00:00:39.340 --> 00:00:40.740
But, in addition:
the way that we have, like,

00:00:40.840 --> 00:00:45.070
eyebrow movements and, like, facial expressions,
they have tail movements,

00:00:45.170 --> 00:00:48.170
and the tail movements are extremely important
in squirrel talking.

00:00:48.270 --> 00:00:51.110
And squirrels, though you may not know this,
have names.

00:00:51.210 --> 00:00:54.359
And each name consists of a vocal component
and a tail movement.

00:00:54.459 --> 00:00:58.999
And this song is about a squirrel
named Scuzzy Kizoozle ... Curl.

00:00:59.099 --> 00:01:00.140
[Irish fiddling]

00:01:10.740 --> 00:01:12.280
[Singing, with Irish accent]
Oh, there's a lot of ways to die,

00:01:12.380 --> 00:01:14.230
Under Ireland's skies,

00:01:14.330 --> 00:01:16.880
But the worst way, by far,
might be a bit of a surprise.

00:01:16.930 --> 00:01:20.100
I'd rather be knifed in a bar,
or hit by a car,

00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:22.660
Than to meet my end 
lookin' into her eyes

00:01:22.760 --> 00:01:24.220
And hear the cries

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:25.755
Of my demise,

00:01:25.855 --> 00:01:30.289
My last goodbyes.

00:01:30.389 --> 00:01:33.430
Oh, she's a six-ounce ball 
of blood, muscle, and fur,

00:01:33.530 --> 00:01:36.500
And she's the evilest thing 
in this or any world.

00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:39.490
And you better start a-runnin' 
if you ever see her;

00:01:39.590 --> 00:01:41.125
She's Scuzzy Kizoozle Curl,

00:01:41.225 --> 00:01:43.359
The homicidal squirrel.

00:01:45.459 --> 00:01:48.390
Well, I was at the county fair,
and Scuzzy Kizoozle was there.

00:01:48.490 --> 00:01:51.680
She was slicing through the crowd,
like a mower mowin' lawns.

00:01:51.780 --> 00:01:54.189
I watched the people fall
to her razor sharp claws,

00:01:54.289 --> 00:01:57.200
And then she was right there in my face,
before I knew what was goin' on.

00:01:57.300 --> 00:01:58.710
I raised my arm,

00:01:58.810 --> 00:02:00.220
